WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.500
Two federal clocks run against this work.

2
00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:07.755
CISA's BOD 26-04, issued June 10,
2026, revoked BOD 22-01 and BOD 19-02,

3
00:00:07.755 --> 00:00:14.398
and replaced severity buckets with four
questions about each vulnerable asset:

4
00:00:14.398 --> 00:00:21.239
is it publicly exposed, is the flaw in the
Known Exploited Vulnerabilities catalog,

5
00:00:21.239 --> 00:00:27.188
can exploitation be automated, and
does it give partial or total control?

6
00:00:27.188 --> 00:00:29.626
The answers set a maximum timeline.

7
00:00:29.626 --> 00:00:33.569
Three days, with forensic triage
on the worst rows, because,

8
00:00:33.569 --> 00:00:38.500
in CISA's words, applying a patch
generally does not evict a threat actor.

9
00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:42.562
Then 14 days, 60 days, or
the next system upgrade.

10
00:00:42.562 --> 00:00:46.851
The clock starts when CISA lists
the flaw or when the agency finds

11
00:00:46.851 --> 00:00:51.062
it and updates CDM, whichever comes
first, and there are no waivers.

12
00:00:51.062 --> 00:00:55.974
Phase III, remediation within the
table, applies from December 7.

13
00:00:55.974 --> 00:01:02.425
M-26-14's clock runs from CISA's
Logging Reference Architecture,

14
00:01:02.425 --> 00:01:08.874
published August 20, 2026: the
Agency Logging Plan within 90 days,

15
00:01:08.874 --> 00:01:14.400
and maturity Levels 1, 2 and 3
within 120, 180 and 320 days.
